<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Suwar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1472">1472</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Şehsuvar Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> was a ruler of the Dulkadirids, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> on the frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. With Ottoman encouragement he rebelled against the Mamluks in the late 1460s and defeated several Mamluk armies. He was finally captured and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1472, and his brother was installed in his place.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> was born before 1432, a son of Suleiman, ruler of Dulkadir from 1442 to 1454. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> had been founded by Zayn al-Din Qaraja as a client of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. When Shah Suwar's brother Malik Arslan was assassinated in October 1465 on the orders of the Mamluk Sultan Khushqadam, another brother, Shah Budak, took the throne with Mamluk support.</p><p>Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> had taken refuge with the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, who made him sanjak-bey of Chirmen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and, on 4 December 1465, governor of Bozok and Artukabad, lands that had belonged to his father. Given authority over the Dulkadir and Bozok nomads, he defeated Shah Budak near the Zamantu Castle and became ruler in April 1466.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> never accepted him. In 1467 he took Birecik, Besni, Gerger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hromkla">Rumkale</a>, burned Sis and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and Ayas; an Armenian colophon of that year accused him of many killings and atrocities. On 4 October 1467 he surprised a Mamluk army at Turnadağ near Göksun and captured its commander, Berdi Beg. On 30 May 1468 he defeated a second army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a> and captured its leader, Janibeg Kulaksiz.</p><p>He then declared himself a sultan equal to the Ottoman ruler, took the title Malik al-Muzaffar, had the khutbah read in his name and struck coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a>, the only Dulkadirid ruler known to have done so. The Mamluk Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> sent further armies. Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> was crushed on the Ceyhan River in April 1469, but by June 1470 he had retaken almost all of the Ramadanid realm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> disapproved of the constant war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and when Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> sheltered the Karamanid ruler Pir Ahmed instead of helping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> against him, Mehmed withdrew his support. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> lords with gold and letters. Defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> on 12 November 1471, Shah Suwar lost his conquests and shut himself in the Castle of Zamantu, where he surrendered on 4 June 1472.</p><p>He was paraded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 24 August 1472 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> had obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> from four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, hanged at Bab Zuweila with his brothers Erdivane, Hudadad and Yahya. He was in his forties. His son Ali ruled Dulkadir from 1515 to 1522 as the last of the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shah Suwar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shah Suwar</b> rebelled against the Mamluks with Ottoman encouragement and was executed in Cairo in 1472.</blockquote><a href="/state/dulkadirids">Dulkadirids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1462" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Shah Suwar"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1462">What was here in 1462? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
